\subsection{Address mapping and four-byte packing}

For list $\ell$, row $r$ maps to
\[
 p=o_\ell+r,\qquad \operatorname{offset}(Z[m,p])=mN+p.
\]
At fixed $m$, $\Delta\operatorname{offset}=\Delta p$. List boundaries can break adjacency.

Pack four subspace bytes per candidate:
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf{w}[j,p]=\sum_{t=0}^{3}2^{8t}Z[4j+t,p],\qquad
 \operatorname{offset}_{\rm bytes}(\mathsf{w}[j,p])=4(jN+p).
 \label{eq:5-packed-layout}
\end{equation}
For $0\leq t<4$ and eight-bit $Z$,
\[
 (\mathsf w[j,p]\gg8t)\mathbin{\&}255=Z[4j+t,p].
\]
Nibbles index \cref{eq:5-lut-factorisation}.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\centering
\input{variants/thesis2/figures/packed-access}
\captionof{figure}[Packed candidate-parallel access]{Packed candidate-parallel access.}
\label{fig:5-packed-access}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Each column is one lane: it reads and unpacks its own word, then uses the four code bytes as LUT indices. Adjacent lanes keep their candidate scores separate. One word load replaces four byte loads per lane, while consecutive lanes read consecutive candidate words.

Construction maps $[M,N]$ bytes to $[M/4,N]$ words ($4\mid M$). Casting would combine candidate bytes. Code-byte costs:
\[
 R=W=NM,\qquad S_{\rm old+new}=2NM.
\]
Repacking is outside search timing.

\subsection{Memory traffic and score consistency}

One word load/unpack replaces four byte loads. The logical warp window is $32\times4=128$ bytes; transactions depend on alignment/active lanes. Payload stays $M$ bytes per candidate. Both tested layouts are subspace-major, so transpose/cache effects are not isolated.

changes.before:
Primary screening changes coordinate work from $O(n_cd)$ to $O(cd)$. Lanes cooperate at the same budget.

\subsection{Reconstructed scoring and the query-only constant}

For $\gamma=2\langle\widehat p,\widehat r\rangle$, expand in real arithmetic:
\begin{align}
 \refineddist(q,x)
 &=\primarydist(q,x)+\|q'-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\gamma(x)
 \label{eq:5-refined-score}\\
 &=2\|q'\|_2^2+\|\widehat p+\widehat r\|_2^2-2\langle q',\widehat p+\widehat r\rangle\nonumber\\
 &=\|q'-\widehat p(x)-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\|q'\|_2^2.
 \label{eq:5-refined-constant}
\end{align}
The query constant preserves reconstructed-distance order \cite{jhq}.

Scalar scoring sums squared residual differences, then adds primary score/correction. Queries and fitting codebooks use shared memory.

\subsection{Mapping lanes to words and reducing partial sums}

For $\lrefine=L\in\{1,8,16,32\}$,
\[
 d_{\rm word}=32/B_R,\qquad
 \text{words of lane }a:\ a,a+L,a+2L,\ldots.
\]
A word holds four eight-bit or eight four-bit codes. Butterfly shuffles exchange and sum partial values between lanes;
lane zero writes the score.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\centering
\input{figures/ch5/lane-refinement}
\captionof{figure}[Sixteen-lane residual refinement]{Sixteen-lane residual refinement. A warp handles two candidates;
lane $a$ within a candidate reads residual words $a,a+16,\ldots$.
Each eight-bit-code word decodes four coordinates. Subgroups sum partials, then add primary score/correction. Candidate positions may remain scattered.}
\label{fig:5-lane-refinement}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Word-aligned rows and $d_{\rm word}\mid D_s$ keep words within codebooks. All lanes shuffle; invalid slots contribute zero, then output infinity. $L=16$ gives two candidates/warp.

L0 loads bytes; L1 loads words with the same one-lane accumulation. L8/L16/L32 trade shorter walks for shuffles/fewer concurrent candidates. FP32 association and locality matter.

\subsection{Final selection and the numerical boundary}

